WEBVTT

00:00:25.001 --> 00:00:32.000
Let us face without panic the reality of
our time, the fact that atom bombs may

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:36.001
someday be dropped on our cities,
and let us prepare for survival by

00:00:36.001 --> 00:00:39.000
understanding the weapon that threatens us.

00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:48.000
An atom bomb destroys or injures in three ways,
by

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:50.000
blast, heat, and radioactivity.

00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:54.000
The blast of an atom bomb is its
most important destructive agent.

00:00:55.000 --> 00:00:57.001
In Japan,
whole buildings were flattened by its force.

00:00:57.001 --> 00:01:01.000
However, many buildings of sturdy construction,
even though close to the

00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:03.000
explosion, remain standing.

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:12.001
The principal dangers of blast are flying
glass and debris, the fires it may

00:01:12.001 --> 00:01:16.000
start, and the danger of being
crushed in collapsing buildings.

00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:22.000
The atom bomb destroys by heat.

00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:26.001
People caught in the open as far as
two miles away suffered flash burns.

00:01:26.001 --> 00:01:29.001
Yet,
protection could have been easily achieved.

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:34.000
Here, a bridge post and rail
shielded the surface behind it.

00:01:34.001 --> 00:01:36.001
Any solid material afforded similar protection.

00:01:39.001 --> 00:01:43.000
The third weapon of the bomb is radioactivity,
thrown

00:01:43.000 --> 00:01:44.001
off at the instant of explosion.

00:01:46.001 --> 00:01:50.001
However, the majority of people exposed
to radiation recovered completely,

00:01:50.001 --> 00:01:54.001
including a large percentage of those
who suffered serious radiation sickness.

00:01:57.001 --> 00:02:02.000
Today, they lead normal lives.
They bear children. Their children are normal.

00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:06.001
These, then, are the weapons of the
atom bomb that we must protect against,

00:02:07.000 --> 00:02:09.000
blast, heat, and radioactivity.

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:16.000
Our cities are prime targets for atomic attack,
but mass

00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:17.001
evacuation would be disastrous.

00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:21.000
An enemy would like nothing
better than to have us leave our

00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:22.001
cities empty and unproductive.

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:27.001
If an emergency should come,
our factories will be battle stations.

00:02:28.001 --> 00:02:30.000
Production must go on if we are to win.

00:02:33.000 --> 00:02:37.000
Our offices and homes will also be
posts of duty, not to be deserted.

00:02:39.001 --> 00:02:44.001
With the knowledge of the first atomic
explosions to guide us, our chances for

00:02:44.001 --> 00:02:48.000
survival will be far better than those
of the people of Hiroshima and Nagasaki,

00:02:48.001 --> 00:02:51.001
if we act on our knowledge and are prepared.

00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:58.001
The first job is to look over your
own home for shelter possibilities.

00:02:59.000 --> 00:03:02.000
If you live in a private
home that is well built, the

00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:04.000
cellar is the safest place to be.

00:03:04.001 --> 00:03:08.000
The lower you get, the more barriers
there are likely to be against

00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:10.000
blast, heat, and radioactivity.

00:03:11.000 --> 00:03:15.000
Select the basement wall nearest the
probable target area of your city.

00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:19.001
If the house is blown over,
it will most likely fall away from this wall.

00:03:20.001 --> 00:03:25.000
If you have a workbench or strong table
big enough to get under, move it into

00:03:25.000 --> 00:03:28.000
your shelter area near a wall
or strong supporting column.

00:03:29.000 --> 00:03:32.001
If the time comes when you actually have
to take shelter, lie under the table.

00:03:38.001 --> 00:03:42.001
If you live in a home that has no basement,
choose a shelter area without

00:03:42.001 --> 00:03:44.000
windows on the ground floor.

00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:47.000
An interior hallway is probably best.

00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:51.001
In time of emergency, the shelter area
should be cleared of mirrors and other

00:03:51.001 --> 00:03:53.000
objects that might cause injury.

00:03:57.001 --> 00:04:01.000
If you live in an apartment house,
rules for taking

00:04:01.000 --> 00:04:02.001
shelter will be posted in your building.

00:04:03.000 --> 00:04:07.000
Learn them. Also,
learn the location of public shelters in areas

00:04:07.000 --> 00:04:08.001
where you work or frequently visit.

00:04:11.001 --> 00:04:16.000
There are many things you
can begin doing right now.

00:04:16.001 --> 00:04:19.000
Fireproof housekeeping is one,
so clean up that attic.

00:04:22.000 --> 00:04:24.001
Keep waste in covered containers.

00:04:26.000 --> 00:04:27.001
Don't let trash pile up in the yard.

00:04:28.001 --> 00:04:32.001
Set aside a small supply of canned goods.
They're safe from radioactivity.

00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:37.001
Have a good flashlight on hand.
Electric lights may go out.

00:04:39.000 --> 00:04:41.001
Keep a first aid kit and
learn how to stop bleeding.

00:04:44.000 --> 00:04:47.000
Make a habit of keeping a
bottle of fresh water handy.

00:04:49.000 --> 00:04:52.000
A radio will be important for
receiving vital instructions.

00:04:54.001 --> 00:04:58.001
Finally, prepare the shelter area and
collect all necessary shelter equipment.

00:05:02.001 --> 00:05:09.000
In case of an actual raid or test drill,
the alert

00:05:09.000 --> 00:05:12.001
will be a warbling siren
blast blasting through the...

00:05:22.000 --> 00:05:24.001
Once you hear this, act fast.

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:36.001
Pull down the shades or blinds and

00:05:36.001 --> 00:05:38.000
close the drapes against flying glass.

00:05:40.001 --> 00:05:42.001
Turn off the burners of
your gas or electric stove.

00:05:44.001 --> 00:05:48.000
Disconnect any heating elements,
such as electric iron, hot plates, or bathroom

00:05:48.000 --> 00:05:49.001
heaters.

00:06:00.001 --> 00:06:06.001
Close all outside doors,
but leave them unlocked.

00:06:09.001 --> 00:06:13.000
Turn off the gas or oil burners.

00:06:35.000 --> 00:06:37.001
Take in shelter may be a race against time,

00:06:38.000 --> 00:06:39.001
even when you have some advance warning.

00:06:40.001 --> 00:06:42.001
But possibly there may be no time.

00:06:43.000 --> 00:06:45.000
An attack could come without warning.

00:06:48.000 --> 00:06:52.000
The sky would suddenly light up.
If a doorway is right at hand, use it.

00:06:52.001 --> 00:06:54.000
If the nearest shelter is
more than a couple of steps

00:06:54.000 --> 00:06:56.000
away, fall to the ground immediately.

00:06:57.000 --> 00:07:00.001
Flying glass and debris are immediate danger,
so stay where you are

00:07:00.001 --> 00:07:02.001
until you're sure it's safe to move.

00:07:11.001 --> 00:07:16.001
If you are at home when a surprise attack
occurs, call beneath a table if it is

00:07:16.001 --> 00:07:20.000
very near, or drop to the floor
with your back to the window.

00:07:29.001 --> 00:07:33.001
The immediate danger is over in about a minute,
unless the explosion

00:07:33.001 --> 00:07:35.001
occurred near the ground or water.

00:07:37.001 --> 00:07:44.000
In this case, radioactive materials
are trapped in the particles of dirt

00:07:44.000 --> 00:07:46.000
or water thrown up by the explosion.

00:07:46.000 --> 00:07:49.000
When these particles fall back to earth,
they may be dangerous.

00:07:49.001 --> 00:07:52.001
So get indoors immediately
after a ground-level explosion.

00:07:53.000 --> 00:07:56.001
Cover broken windows against radioactive
dust with blankets or cardboard.

00:07:59.000 --> 00:08:05.001
Civil defense radiological teams equipped
with radiation survey meters will check

00:08:05.001 --> 00:08:08.000
on contamination in any bombed area.

00:08:08.001 --> 00:08:12.000
Stay under cover until you hear
officially that it is safe outside.

00:08:13.001 --> 00:08:18.000
If you have been exposed to radioactive dust,
wash the exposed areas.

00:08:18.001 --> 00:08:21.000
Pay particular attention to your hair. Get

00:08:21.000 --> 00:08:22.001
all the dirt from under your fingernails.

00:08:24.000 --> 00:08:28.001
If the people of Hiroshima and Nagasaki
had known what we know about civil

00:08:28.001 --> 00:08:31.001
defense,
thousands of lives would have been saved.

00:08:32.001 --> 00:08:37.001
Yes, the knowledge is ours,
and preparation can mean survival for you.

00:08:38.000 --> 00:08:41.001
So act now. Someday your life may depend on it.

00:08:42.000 --> 00:08:59.000
For more information, visit www.fema.gov
